WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:04.400
As long as developers have made video games,
they've hidden secret Easter eggs in them

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.390
for their own entertainment, or as a practical
joke.

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:11.660
Most of the time, these Easter eggs are intended
to be found, but sometimes features in a game

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:16.030
may be scrapped before the title hits the
shelves, or unfinished content is left inside

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:17.940
for anyone smart enough to find it.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.590
Here's a look at some video game secrets you
were never supposed to discover.

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:24.470
The mysterious dam in Shadow of the Colossus

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:27.560
This PlayStation 2 adventure title is a cult
classic.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.350
But developers cut a few things from the final
version of Shadow of the Colossus — but

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.160
still left them in the code.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.870
One is a huge dam that some intrepid hackers
found.

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:39.980
The structure is immense and looks pretty
complete, but nobody knows what it was intended

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:40.980
for.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:42.170
Maybe it was meant to house a colossus?

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:44.350
Or… possibly just its shadow?

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:46.090
It's a mystery.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.570
All Bonds mode in Goldeneye

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:52.520
If the team at Rare Games hadn't run into
some licensing difficulties, you might have

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:57.500
been able to play as other classic Bond actors
besides Pierce Brosnan in the classic Nintendo

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.320
64 game GoldenEye.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:04.220
That's right, the original version of Goldeneye
came with models and textures for Sean Connery,

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:08.100
Roger Moore, and Timothy Dalton, which you
could use in "All Bonds" mode to play the

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.100
bonus Egypt and Aztec levels, or in multiplayer.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.370
When the developers learned the mode had to
be scrapped, they held a three-hour deathmatch

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:16.500
to send it off in style.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.140
Hey, if you've got a license to kill, you
might as well use it.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.380
"That gun… looks more fitting for a woman."

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.590
South Park pilot in Tiger Woods 99 PGA Tour

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:31.060
On the PlayStation 2 version of Tiger Woods
99 PGA Tour, a developer filled out the extra

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.340
space on the disc with a "dummy" file.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:37.159
But instead of what should have been empty
space, the file contained a five-minute video

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:40.150
of the South Park pilot episode, "Jesus vs.
Santa."

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:44.280
While you couldn't view the episode in your
PlayStation, sticking the disc in a PC and

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.580
using File Explorer to open the video would
allow you to watch the entire pilot in it

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:50.200
all its vulgar glory.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.450
Naturally, this caused an uproar among the
parents who had purchased the child-friendly

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:58.770
title, so EA recalled the game, replacing
it with a version that had the dummy file

00:01:58.770 --> 00:01:59.770
removed.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:02.380
The hidden mountain observatory in BioShock
Infinite

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.579
Near the end of the BioShock Infinite, players
are led to an area with a lot of lighthouses

00:02:06.579 --> 00:02:07.670
in an endless ocean.

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:11.880
It's a trippy sight, but if you're on a PC
and able to turn off the game's clipping — which

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.210
allows you to walk through objects — you
can fly through the ocean and land in mountainous

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:17.210
terrain.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:21.220
There are snowy cliffs and a bridge that leads
to an observatory, in an area that looks straight

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:22.330
out of Skyrim.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.020
When you explore the observatory, you'll find
a purple door.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:29.880
But if you open that door, you fall into an
infinite abyss and get lost in the ether.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.090
So… maybe don't open the door.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:35.270
The unused worlds in Kingdom Hearts: Birth
by Sleep

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:39.209
Beloved for their amazing visuals, the Kingdom
Hearts games allow players to explore their

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:41.810
favorite Disney and Square Enix worlds.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:45.850
But a few of those worlds were cut from from
Kingdom Hearts: Birth by Sleep after basic

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:50.349
versions had already been created, such as
the unfinished Jungle Book world.

00:02:50.349 --> 00:02:53.980
While there aren't many backgrounds, there
are lots of cool structures and terrains,

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:56.880
including King Louie's temple, complete with
his throne.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:57.880
Cool.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:00.599
Adult minigame in Grand Theft Auto: San Andreas

00:03:00.599 --> 00:03:05.060
While the Grand Theft Auto franchise is designed
for older teens and adults, some players got

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:09.290
more than they bargained for when some gamers
discovered a built-in sex minigame that was

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:13.709
buried in its code and cut from the finished
version of GTA: San Andreas.

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:17.490
Called the "Hot Coffee" mod, players could
follow up a successful date with their girlfriend

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.910
by playing a mini-game where you have to you
have to control your character's rhythm and

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:25.739
even change sexual positions to fill up the
"excitement" bar, like some kind of bizarre

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.300
X-rated version of Guitar Hero.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:32.260
"Inappropriate!"

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:37.360
Naturally, parents and ratings boards freaked
out when they learned of the minigame, which

00:03:37.379 --> 00:03:41.020
caused the ESRB to re-rate San Andreas "Adults
Only."

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:44.990
The developers recalled the game and issued
a new version which removed all the content

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:49.340
from the minigame, and also created a patch
to disable it in PC versions.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.400
That wasn't enough to completely save them
from the fallout, however, and Take Two ended

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:57.830
up paying out over a million dollars in class-action
lawsuits and other civil suits related to

00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:58.830
the mod.

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:03.720
Today, original copies of the game are collector's
items, and a new sealed copy of the original

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:07.900
"Hot Coffee" capable San Andreas can sell
for $75 or more.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:11.629
Dismemberment mode in Star Wars Jedi Knight:
Jedi Academy

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:15.400
Considering how deadly lightsabers are, you'd
think more people would actually get killed

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:16.900
by them in Star Wars games.

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:18.669
But usually, you just can't do that.

00:04:18.669 --> 00:04:23.169
Unless, of course, you know the cheat code
for the original version of Jedi Knight: Jedi

00:04:23.169 --> 00:04:25.629
Academy that unlocks Dismemberment Mode.

00:04:25.629 --> 00:04:29.349
Unsurprisingly, Dismemberment Mode allows
players to hack their opponents up with a

00:04:29.349 --> 00:04:30.349
lightsaber.

00:04:30.349 --> 00:04:34.069
Just be careful: once the mode is turned on,
the player can also be dismembered.

00:04:34.069 --> 00:04:36.599
Of course, there are benefits to going out
like that.

00:04:36.599 --> 00:04:43.050
"If you strike me down, I shall become more
powerful than you can possibly imagine."

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:45.229
Minus World in Super Mario Bros.

00:04:45.229 --> 00:04:49.120
As you probably know, each level in Super
Mario Bros. is numbered according to the world

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.580
you're in: 1-1, 2-1, etc.

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:54.270
But did you know there's actually a "Minus
World?"

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:57.490
That's right, if you know the right trick
you can reach a world that's simply numbered

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:00.909
minus one, which is how it got its nickname
among players.

00:05:00.909 --> 00:05:03.180
Just what you'll get in Minus World depends
on your platform.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.770
On the NES, you'll get sent to a world that
that looks identical to underwater world 7-2,

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:11.369
but the level can't be finished, as the final
pipe will simply send you back to the start

00:05:11.369 --> 00:05:12.369
of the level.

00:05:12.369 --> 00:05:16.910
In the Japanese Famicom version, though, finishing
Minus World brings you to Minus-Two World,

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:21.100
and then Minus-Three World, before restarting
the entire game in hard mode.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.759
MissingNo. in Pokemon Red and Blue

00:05:23.759 --> 00:05:27.509
MissingNo is the "hidden" Pokemon that players
can find in Pokemon Red and Blue.

00:05:27.509 --> 00:05:31.490
You can obtain it through a number of glitches
in the game — one being the infamous "old

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:32.490
man" glitch.

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:35.990
First, talk to the old man north of Viridian
City and get him to show you how to catch

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:37.309
a Pokemon.

00:05:37.309 --> 00:05:41.699
After the demonstration, fly to Cinnabar Island
and use surf to travel up and down the eastern

00:05:41.699 --> 00:05:42.699
coast.

00:05:42.699 --> 00:05:46.649
You'll encounter a number of Pokemon, with
a chance to find MissingNo.

00:05:46.649 --> 00:05:50.009
Because it's a glitched Pokemon, your game
experience could get wonky.

00:05:50.009 --> 00:05:53.809
But weird pixels and strange item duplications
are a small price to pay for catching the

00:05:53.809 --> 00:05:56.419
infamous glitch-Pokemon MissingNo.

00:05:56.419 --> 00:05:59.639
Unfinished world in Final Fantasy XV: Episode
Duscae

00:05:59.639 --> 00:06:04.539
Before Final Fantasy XV officially hit the
shelves at the end of November 2016, Square

00:06:04.539 --> 00:06:09.729
Enix made a large playable demo titled Episode
Duscae available via an exclusive download

00:06:09.729 --> 00:06:10.979
code in 2015.

00:06:10.979 --> 00:06:15.169
It included a glowing blue barrier which kept
you from leaving the demo area.

00:06:15.169 --> 00:06:19.509
Unless, of course, you figured out the in-game
exploit: if you got hit by a car near the

00:06:19.509 --> 00:06:23.069
barrier, it would bounce you right through
to the other side, where a giant world of

00:06:23.069 --> 00:06:25.240
dinosaurs and Titans awaits.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:28.979
Unfortunately, if you did anything that would
auto-save your game, it would corrupt the

00:06:28.979 --> 00:06:32.461
file, but hey, that's the risk you take when
you're a maverick — or when you're trying

00:06:32.461 --> 00:06:34.160
to do your best Terminator impression.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:43.120
"Please remain calm."

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:46.099
Nightmare sequence in Freddi Fish

00:06:46.099 --> 00:06:50.919
In this "Junior Adventure" for ages 3-8, you
play as Freddi, a plucky little yellow fish,

00:06:50.919 --> 00:06:54.659
who is accompanied by his best buddy, Luther,
on a mission to track down the thieves who

00:06:54.659 --> 00:06:56.300
stole the kelp seeds.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:00.490
Sounds charming — right up until you enter
a cheat code that triggers a nightmare world

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:05.020
where Freddi offers Luther up as a sacrifice
to Eddie the Eel, who gleefully eats Luther

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:06.360
alive.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:10.960
Turns out it's just Freddi's twisted daydream,
but… that's not actually any better.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:14.749
Lord of the Pies in Dragon Age: Inquisition

00:07:14.749 --> 00:07:19.110
As awesome as Dragon Age: Inquisition is,
it also contains more than a few bugs and

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:23.349
glitches, one of which causes you to occasionally
fall through the floor of your castle stronghold.

00:07:23.349 --> 00:07:27.149
But while BioWare didn't actually fix this
problem, they did at least hide an Easter

00:07:27.149 --> 00:07:32.319
egg down there in the form of "Lord of the
Pies," a huge smiling pie wearing a tophat.

00:07:32.319 --> 00:07:36.869
Because if you can't have quality control,
at least you can have a terrifying pie.

00:07:36.869 --> 00:07:39.249
Developer test level in Sonic Adventure 2

00:07:39.249 --> 00:07:43.479
When Sonic Adventure 2: Battle first came
out for GameCube, fans soon discovered that

00:07:43.479 --> 00:07:48.089
you could access a secret developer test level
by using the correct code on an Action Replay

00:07:48.089 --> 00:07:49.089
device.

00:07:49.089 --> 00:07:54.020
When the game was ported to PS3 and Xbox 360
in 2012, the developers never bothered to

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:57.650
remove the secret level — probably because
it's actually pretty cool.

00:07:57.650 --> 00:08:01.499
There's not a lot to it, but it's still a
fun little area to visit and play around with

00:08:01.499 --> 00:08:03.110
the physics of Sonic.

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:05.939
The secret brothel in Final Fantasy VII

00:08:05.939 --> 00:08:10.259
The Honey Bee Inn in Final Fantasy VII is
a building where lonely men seek companionship

00:08:10.259 --> 00:08:11.959
from ladies in bee costumes.

00:08:11.959 --> 00:08:16.289
While players can only access a few rooms,
there are a few areas that were only accessible

00:08:16.289 --> 00:08:18.419
in the original Japanese release.

00:08:18.419 --> 00:08:22.189
Among the hidden areas are an employee room,
waiting room, and lobby.

00:08:22.189 --> 00:08:25.909
These rooms even feature characters that had
completed dialogue, but are never seen in

00:08:25.909 --> 00:08:27.279
the main game.

00:08:27.279 --> 00:08:31.059
You know, maybe it's for the best that young
gamers weren't allowed to explore a bee themed

00:08:31.059 --> 00:08:32.320
brothel.

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:34.759
The Netherspace in World of Warcraft

00:08:34.759 --> 00:08:38.829
For over a decade, intrepid World of Warcraft
players have been finding ways to exploit

00:08:38.829 --> 00:08:43.279
the sometimes buggy walls and ceilings of
the Karazhan raid in order to slip outside

00:08:43.279 --> 00:08:44.380
the castle.

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:47.880
Once you're out, there's lots of things to
do, like explore a nearby Ogre village, or

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.040
view the smiley face left by developers.

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:53.700
The main draw, though, is the unfinished zone
called Netherspace.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:57.480
Not only can you explore some big floating
rocks in the sky, you can also use a bug to

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:01.970
walk right up the wall and get to the last
boss, skipping the entire massive Karazhan

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:02.970
raid.

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:06.610
For anyone who ever suffered through the Chess
Match sequence, this is one glitch just begging

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:07.750
to be exploited.

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:10.220
The secret treasure trove in Fallout 4

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:14.870
In the PC version of Fallout 4, you can access
a secret room that houses every item in the

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:17.079
game by entering a secret developer code.

00:09:17.079 --> 00:09:21.430
Inside, there are footlockers, cooking stations,
crafting stations, a terminal, and enough

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:24.400
rows of power armor suits to make Iron Man
jealous.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:28.220
We recommend loading up on everything you
can while strapping on the X-01 power armor,

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:29.380
the best in the game.

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:32.470
You might look like a hoarder, but no one
will say anything when you're rocking that

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:33.920
power armor.

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:36.639
Equipment room in The Elder Scrolls V: Skyrim

00:09:36.639 --> 00:09:40.920
Just like they did in Fallout 4, the folks
at Bethesda also hid a secret equipment room

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:44.920
in Skyrim, which you can access on PC by entering
some cheat codes.

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:48.829
That will teleport you to a room where all
your item-hoarding dreams can come true.

00:09:48.829 --> 00:09:52.240
There's one catch, though: there are so many
items in the room that simply loading the

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:55.639
loot list when you open a chest can crash
some computers.

00:09:55.639 --> 00:09:59.160
Now we know why HAL wouldn't open that door
— he knew the other side was filled with

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:00.430
magic axes.

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:03.200
"Open the pod bay doors, HAL."

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:06.220
"I'm sorry, Dave.

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:08.420
I'm afraid I can't do that."

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:10.540
Thanks for watching!

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:13.160
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:15.299
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

